{"text": "K Leverette-\n\n\u00a9 1996 Oxford Universi Press Hci Acids Research, 1996, Vo. 24, No.1\n\nSomatic point mutations in the p53 gene of human\ntumors and cell lines: updated compilation\n\nM. Holistein*, B. Shomer!, M. Greenblatt2, T. Soussi\u00ae, E. Hovig\u2019, R. Montesano\u2019 and\nC.C. Harris\u00ae\n\nGerman Cancer Research Center, Im Neuenheimer Feld 280,0-69120 Heidelberg, Germany, \"EMBL.\nCutstation-Eurepean Bioinformatics Institute, Hinxton Hall, Hinxton, Cambridge, UK, 2University of Vermont\nCollege of Medicina, Healtn Sciences Complex, Burlingion, VT 05405, USA, Sinsttut de Genetique Mol\u00e9culaire,\nUnite 201 INSERM, 27 tue Juliette Dodu, 75010 Paris, France, \u201cinstitute for Cancer Research, Norwegian\nRadium Hospital, 0310 Osto, Norway, Sintemational Agency for Research on Cancer, 150 cours Albert Thomas,\n\nM16\n\n69972 Lyon Cedex 08, France and Laboratory of\nMD 20892, USA\n\nFeceved October 1, 1988, Accoped October 16, 1995,\n\nABSTRACT\n\ntn 1994 we described alist of -2500 point mutations in\nthe p53 gene of human tumors and cell lines which we\nhad compiled from the published fiteature and mace\navailable electronically through the fle server at the\nEMBL Data Library. This database, updated twice a\nyear, now contains records on 4496 published mute-\ntions (July 1985 release) and can be obtained from the\nEMBL Outstation-the European Bioinformatics insti-\ntute (EB!) through the network or on CD-ROM. This\nreport describes the criteria for inclusion of data inthis\ndatabase, a description of the current format and a\nbriet discussion of the current relevance of p53\nmutation analysis to clinical and biological questions.\n\nINTRODUCTION\n\n\u201cThe p33 tumor suppressor gene encodes a phosphoprotein with\ncancer inhibiting functions. Development of cancer in humans\n\"ypically involves loss of wild.\u00abype p53 activity and consequently\n\u2018ofthe growth control it confers. Point mulation sa common route\nto this loss of wild-type p83 function in tumors, Since the\ndiscovery in 1989 by Vogelstein and colleagues of tumor-spe-\ncific, missense mutations in the 53 gene of two patients with\ncolorectal cancer there has been an exponential increase in the\nnumber of p53 mutations identified in human tumors. which Wi\nsurpass the 5000 mark by the end of 1995 (Fig 1). Simple\ninspection ofthese data. such as analysis ofthe frequency, kind\n\u2018and distribution of mutations slong the p53 gene, can be used to\n\u2018make inferences abou potentially important sources of mutation\nin the human setting. The mutanons are also unformative with\nregardto the biochemistry of interactions between the p53 protein\nand its macromolecular targets and the biological factors\ncontributing to selection of mutant clones during tumorigenesis\n\nian Carcinogenesis, National Cancer Institute, Bethesda,\n\nMutations in the Database\n\neEPBEZE\nYear\n\nFigure 1 Huron tomor mations he 953 gene publabed sce 1989\n\nin different tissues. Finally, p53 mutations have a potently\nimportant clinieal role in diagnostic and therapeutic procedures\n\n\u2018SCOPE OF THE MUTATION COMPILATION AND\nCRITERIA FOR INCLUSION OF DATA\n\n\u2018The purpose of our database isto reieve and arange data from\nthe accurmulating iterature on p53 mutations in a standardized\nelectronic format. This provides a powerful means for manpula-\ntion, comparison, search and retrieval of records describing the\nnature of p53 mutations in various cancers. Software which\nenables more sophisticated mutation spectra analysis is also\navailable (1). Inthe forticoming year updates of our database\n\u2018may also be available from EBI in Microsoft Access\u2122 format to\n\n\u201cTo whom compondence ould te akewed\n\n2SIE19h6", "words": [{"text": "K", "x0": 520, "y0": 93, "x1": 541, "y1": 121, "conf": 50.0}, {"text": "Leverette-", "x0": 550, "y0": 93, "x1": 655, "y1": 129, "conf": 36.0}, {"text": "\u00a9", "x0": 68, "y0": 132, "x1": 76, "y1": 141, "conf":